%S A400857 28,52,100,104,112,117,148,172,182,196,208,225,268,272,292,316,333,
%T A400857 344,351,364,372,388,392,400,416,423,448,456,508,513,518,536,544,549,
%U A400857 556,567,568,572,584,592,602,632,652,657,665,675,676,688,700,708,728,772,777
%N A400857 Numbers m in A396684 such that there does not exist k such that the multiplicative group of squares modulo k is cyclic of order m.
%C A400857 Write Q(m) = {k : the multiplicative group of squares modulo k is cyclic of order m}, then Q(1) = {k : (Z/kZ)* = (C_2)^r} = {divisors of 24}; for m > 1, k is in Q(m) if and only if (Z/kZ)* is of the form (C_2)^r X C_{2m}.
%C A400857 Factor m = p_1*...*p_s > 1 into prime powers. Then numbers in Q(m) are of the form d*q_{S_1}*...*q_{S_t}, where S_1, ..., S_t is a partition of {1, 2, ..., s}, q_{S_i} is a prime power such that (Z/q_{S_i})* = C_{2*Product_{j in S_i} p_j} or C_2 X C_{2*Product_{j in S_i} p_j}, and d is a divisor of 24 coprime to q_{S_1}*...*q_{S_t}.
%H A400857 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400857/b400857.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400857 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852.txt">Examples showing the procedure to find Q(m)</a>.
%H A400857 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852_1.txt">List of Q(m) for the first 10000 values of m in A396684</a>.
%H A400857 Jianing Song, <a href="/A400852/a400852_2.txt">PARI program for A400852</a>.
%H A400857 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multiplicative_group_of_integers_modulo_n">Multiplicative group of integers modulo n</a>.
%e A400857 28 is a term since there is no k such that the multiplicative group of squares modulo k is cyclic of order 28; in other words, there is no k such that (Z/kZ)* is of the form (C_2)^r X C_56. See Links.
%o A400857 (PARI) isA400857(m) = is(2*m) && (#multgp(m) == 0) \\ See _Charles R Greathouse IV_'s second program in A002174 and the PARI program link for multgp
%Y A400857 Cf. A400852 (cardinality of Q(m)), A400856 (complement in A396684).
%Y A400857 Cf. A396684.
%K A400857 nonn
